  "slug": "mario-christian-meyer",
  "summary": "Mario-Christian Meyer is a Swiss-Brazilian physician who works to preserve Amazonian biodiversity and Indigenous cultural knowledge by linking traditional expertise with Western science and biotechnology.\n\nAfter training in medicine and specializing in developmental neuropsychology and child psychiatry, Meyer taught at the Sorbonne. A UNESCO study of Indigenous literacy brought him to the Amazon, where research into language and cultural practices shaped his later advocacy. He joined the Amazonas delegation to the 1992 Rio Earth Summit and coordinated a France-Amazonas biotechnology mission in 1994. In 1999, UNESCO asked him to propose ways to support equitable cooperation between Indigenous communities and Western researchers.\n\nMeyer founded PISAD, a nonprofit focused on sustainable development, and developed the Herb’Içana project. It aims to transfer plant-based biotechnology to Indigenous communities so they can produce medicinal ingredients while gaining economic autonomy. His work also promotes the preservation of ancestral knowledge and rainforest biodiversity.\n\nMeyer received Brazil’s Giant of Ecology awards in 2008 and 2017, was named a Knight of the French Order of Merit in 2017, and became an honorary citizen of Manaus.",
